package chat

// The chat surface: the log, the compose form, and the export control.
//
// # Why chat is one of the few justified live regions
//
// UI §7.5 gives the play page's sidebar a live region for the chat line, and the
// same section says the **search route must have none**. Those two facts are one
// rule, and the rule is about whether the content changes *while the reader is
// looking at it*:
//
//   - **Chat qualifies** because a message arrives at a reader who did nothing to
//     ask for it. Somebody else types. The alternative — polling — is a request
//     every reader on the table makes forever to learn that nothing happened, and a
//     `role="log"` is the one construct that exists for "a list that grows while
//     nobody is interacting with it". The log is also the only region on this
//     surface whose *content* is the announcement: a reader who missed a line
//     cannot find it by looking again, because the scrollback has moved past it.
//   - **Search must not have one** because a search result list is reached by
//     navigation. The reader submitted the query; the response is a document; the
//     result count is in the `<h1>` (§7.5). A live region there would fire on every
//     load of that URL, and — worse — a *type-ahead* live region would read the
//     reader's own keystrokes back to them as they type, which is the opposite of
//     what a live region is for.
//
// So the contrast is not "chat is special and search is not". It is **arrival is
// announced; delivery is not.** A chat line is an event. A search result is a
// document. This file is one half of that sentence and
// `internal/httpapi/search/results.templ` is the other, and the test that holds
// both is `TestTheChatSurfaceCarriesALiveRegionAndTheSearchSurfaceDoesNot` — one
// predicate, two documents, because a rule only one route could violate is not a
// rule.
//
// # Why the history and the live log are two elements
//
// §7.5's other rule for this surface is the sharpest in the file: "the chat and
// dice logs **must not replay history into a live region on connect** — a user
// joining mid-table would otherwise hear the whole table read aloud."
//
// The two ways to satisfy that are both wrong on their own. `aria-live="off"` on
// the log means it never announces anything, because the attribute is read from the
// document and nothing in this phase rewrites it. Turning it to `polite` from
// script means the reader's first half-second after the script loads is decided by
// whether the script has run yet.
//
// So the markup is split: `ChatHistory` carries the messages that arrived **with
// the document**, marked `aria-live="off"` so they are never announced; `ChatLog` is
// the region, `role="log"` with `aria-live="polite"` and `aria-relevant="additions"`,
// and it is **empty at load**. The client appends arriving messages into it. A
// reader who has never had script hears the whole table; a reader with script hears
// only what arrives after they did. Neither case requires the other half of the
// contract to hold.
//
// `aria-atomic="false"` for the same reason: with it, an appended `<li>` is
// announced as itself rather than as the whole log read out again from the top.
//
// # `role="log"`, and where §7.5's `role="status"` went
//
// §7.5's table names `role="status"` for a chat line, and this uses `role="log"`.
// The two are not interchangeable and the difference is the whole reason this is
// written down rather than just done:
//
//   - `role="status"` is a **live region that is not a container**. Putting it on
//     the `<ol>` announces every child as a change to the region as a whole, and
//     putting it on each `<li>` is N live regions for one conversation.
//   - `role="log"` is a container **whose implicit `aria-live` is `polite`**. It is
//     defined for exactly this: a list of messages added over time. It also carries
//     the semantics a screen reader uses to offer "read from here", which a status
//     region does not.
//
// `aria-live="polite"` is written out explicitly even though `log` implies it,
// because the gate and the next reader both look at the attribute and an implicit
// politeness is invisible to both.
//
// The role deviation from §7.5's table is a **record change, not a code change**:
// this file's PR description asks the integrator for a note in the UI record's
// channel section. It is not an ADR — the design records are immutable, and
// AGENTS.md's rule is that a correction is an entry in the design index's "Known
// staleness" or an ADR, neither of which this work item owns the path to.
//
// # What the reader sees, and what is not said
//
// Every message body is campaign content and goes through templ's escaper; there is
// no `templ.Raw` anywhere in this package, and a chat message is the one piece of
// this surface a reader cannot be trusted to have authored. §10.2's vocabulary rule
// is therefore scoped to *this package's* copy — see `chat_test.go`, which is where
// that scoping is written down and mutation-checked.
//
// Secret messages are **absent**, not marked and not classed. `Message` is only
// ever handed a message the caller has already decided this reader may see, and the
// test for that is in `internal/realtime`, where the omission happens.

import (
	"strconv"
	"time"

	"github.com/semiplane/semiplane/internal/web/components"
)

// The test hooks, in one place.
//
// Named rather than written inline for the reason `ui.headingID` derives heading
// ids from them: §10.2's hook rule asserts every `data-testid` appears exactly once
// and is not empty, so a hook that is spelled two ways in two files is a hook that
// appears twice and fails the audit for a reason nobody would guess. Each is also
// the id the `aria-labelledby` that names the panel's heading resolves to, so a
// rename has to move both ends and the audit is what catches the one that did not.
const (
	// PanelTestID is the panel's `<section>`.
	PanelTestID = "chat-panel"
	// PanelHeadingID is the panel's heading, which `Panel`'s
	// `aria-labelledby` names.
	PanelHeadingID = "chat-panel-heading"
	// HistoryTestID is the list of messages that arrived with the document.
	HistoryTestID = "chat-history"
	// HistoryHeadingID is the history's own heading, and the live log's
	// `aria-labelledby`.
	HistoryHeadingID = "chat-history-heading"
	// LogTestID is the live region the client appends into.
	LogTestID = "chat-log"
	// LogTestIDHook is the `data-chrome` selector the client layer hooks, kept
	// separate from the test hook because they are different contracts: one is
	// asserted by the a11y gate and the other is a promise to phase 9's script.
	LogChromeHook = "chat-log"
	// ComposeTestID is the form a reader types into.
	ComposeTestID = "chat-compose"
	// ComposeFieldID is the textarea's id, which the form's `<label for>` names.
	ComposeFieldID = "chat-compose-body"
	// ComposeFieldTestID is the textarea's test hook.
	ComposeFieldTestID = "chat-compose-body"
	// ComposeHintID is the hint's id, which the field's `aria-describedby` names.
	ComposeHintID = "chat-compose-hint"
	// ComposeErrorID is the refusal's id, appended to the field's
	// `aria-describedby` when there is one.
	ComposeErrorID = "chat-compose-error"
	// ComposeErrorTestID is the refusal's test hook.
	ComposeErrorTestID = "chat-compose-error"
	// ComposeSubmitTestID is the send button.
	ComposeSubmitTestID = "chat-compose-send"
	// ExportTestID is the "export to journal" control.
	ExportTestID = "chat-export"
	// EmptyTestID is the no-messages-yet state.
	EmptyTestID = "chat-empty"
	// MessageCountTestID is the panel's own count line.
	MessageCountTestID = "chat-message-count"
	// StateTestID is the panel's `<section>`'s state hook, carrying the empty or
	// populated state so a test asserts one rather than inferring it from a count.
	StateTestID = "chat-state"
)

// Message is one chat line as the interface sees it.
//
// A separate type rather than `realtime.Message` for the reason
// `components.WikiPageView` holds a rendered body rather than a domain type: this
// package must not import `internal/realtime`, so that the wire codec, the hub and
// the interface can change independently, and so a template is never handed a
// value with fields nobody has decided a reader may see.
//
// The consequence is that the **decision to omit a secret message happens before
// this type exists** — in the route, from `Chat.Visible`. A message that reached a
// `Message` has already been judged visible, and there is no `Secret` field
// here for a template to forget to check.
type Message struct {
	// Seq is the message's position in the campaign's chat, and the value a
	// reader's client stores as its cursor. Rendered as `data-seq` on the row so a
	// fragment arriving over SSE can be reconciled without parsing prose.
	Seq uint64
	// Author is who said it.
	Author string
	// Body is the text. Already decided visible by the caller; escaped here.
	Body string
	// At is when it was said. Carried in UTC from the buffer so the rendering does
	// not depend on the reader's timezone.
	At time.Time
}

// ComposeView is the form a reader sends a message with.
type ComposeView struct {
	// Action is where the form posts.
	Action string
	// Value is what is in the textarea. Set after a refused send so a reader does
	// not have to retype, and the reason `internal/httpapi/edit` hands the GM's own
	// buffer back on a 412.
	Value string
	// Placeholder is the field's prompt. Empty renders no attribute rather than an
	// empty one.
	Placeholder string
	// Max is the byte ceiling the server enforces (`realtime.MaxChatBodyBytes`),
	// written onto the field so the browser refuses before the request does. The
	// server's check is the one that counts; this is a courtesy.
	Max int
	// Error is a refusal to send, rendered as §4.7's load-error shape under the
	// form rather than as an alert: a message the server would not take is not an
	// emergency.
	Error string
	// SignedIn reports whether there is an account to send as. False renders the
	// form as a paragraph rather than a disabled one: a textarea a reader cannot
	// use is a focus stop that does nothing.
	SignedIn bool
}

// ExportView is the "export to journal" control and what it last did.
type ExportView struct {
	// Href is where the control posts.
	Href string
	// Allowed reports whether this reader may export at all. Content writing is
	// GM-only (S-8) and an export writes a page, so a player sees the count and not
	// the control — the same line `internal/httpapi/edit` draws between the editor
	// and the reader.
	Allowed bool
	// Withheld is how many secret messages were left out of the last export, and is
	// zero when there was no export. Shown because a GM who pressed the button is
	// entitled to know a line is not in the page.
	Withheld int
	// LastPath is the page the last export wrote, as a root-relative path. Shown
	// because a transcript whose location nobody was told about is a transcript
	// nobody will find.
	LastPath string
}

// PanelView is the whole panel, as the play page's sidebar mounts it.
type PanelView struct {
	// Messages are the retained messages, oldest first, already filtered for this
	// reader.
	Messages []Message
	// Truncated reports that the ring has evicted messages this reader's session did
	// not see. Rendered as a note above the list, because the alternative is a
	// transcript that reads as complete and is not.
	Truncated bool
	// Compose is the send form. Its zero value renders the signed-out paragraph.
	Compose ComposeView
	// Export is the export control. Its zero value renders nothing.
	Export ExportView
}

// PageView is the centre slot: the `<h1>` plus the panel.
//
// Separate from `PanelView` for the reason `components.WikiPageView` and
// `components.WikiArticle` are two: the heading belongs to the route and the states
// below it carry none of their own, so a reader who lands on a panel with no
// messages is still told where they are.
type PageView struct {
	// Shell is the chrome. Its `Instance` field also fills the right rail.
	Shell components.ShellView
	// Heading is what the page calls itself.
	Heading string
	// Panel is the panel itself.
	Panel PanelView
}

// Page is the document around the chat panel.
//
// `components.Shell` rather than `components.CampaignShell`, and the reason is the
// one `internal/httpapi/edit` gives: the campaign shell wants a `chrome.NavView`,
// and the left navigation's skip link must be present exactly where the navigation
// is (§7.2). A hand-built `NavView` would render a navigation landmark with
// nothing in it, which §4.6 forbids and which would also mean the skip link and the
// landmark were two independent decisions.
templ Page(view PageView) {
	@components.Shell(view.Shell, PanelPage(view), components.InstanceRail(view.Shell.Instance))
}

// PanelPage is the centre slot's content: the heading, then the panel.
templ PanelPage(view PageView) {
	<h1 id="chat-page-heading" data-testid="chat-page-title">{ view.Heading }</h1>
	@Panel(view.Panel)
}

// Panel is the chat surface: a section named by its own heading, holding the log,
// the compose form, and the export control.
//
// A `<section>` with `aria-labelledby` rather than a `<div>`, and the label is the
// heading **inside** the section rather than a hidden span. §7.2's rule is that a
// landmark's name resolves to something a screen reader can announce, and a visible
// heading is the only version of that which is also visible to everyone else.
templ Panel(view PanelView) {
	<section
		class="panel chat"
		aria-labelledby={ PanelHeadingID }
		data-state={ panelState(view.Messages) }
		data-testid={ PanelTestID }
	>
		<h2 id={ PanelHeadingID }>Chat</h2>
		<p class="panel-note" data-testid={ MessageCountTestID }>{ countLabel(view.Messages) }</p>
		@ChatHistory(view)
		@Compose(view.Compose)
		@Export(view.Export)
		// The live region. **Empty at load**, which is the whole of §7.5's replay
		// rule: the messages above are already on the screen and must not be
		// announced, and only what arrives after this document is live.
		<ol
			class="card-list chat-log"
			role="log"
			aria-live="polite"
			aria-atomic="false"
			aria-relevant="additions"
			aria-labelledby={ HistoryHeadingID }
			data-chrome={ LogChromeHook }
			data-testid={ LogTestID }
		></ol>
	</section>
}

// ChatHistory is the messages that arrived with the document.
//
// **`aria-live="off"` and not no attribute at all**, and the distinction is worth a
// sentence: an element with no `aria-live` inherits the politeness of its nearest
// live-region ancestor, and this component is mounted inside a page that has other
// regions in it. Stating `off` is what makes "these are not announced" a property
// of this element rather than a property of wherever it was placed — which is the
// whole difference between a contract and an environment.
//
// The heading is a `<h3>`: this is a region inside the panel's `<h2>`, and §7.2's
// rule that a heading level may not skip is about the reader's outline rather than
// about the HTML, so the levels are contiguous.
templ ChatHistory(view PanelView) {
	<h3 id={ HistoryHeadingID } class="visually-hidden">What has been said</h3>
	if view.Truncated {
		// Omission is the property this note exists for: it says the list starts
		// partway through and does not pretend the earlier lines are coming back.
		// The ring has evicted them and nothing will restore them.
		<p class="empty-note" data-testid="chat-history-truncated">
			Earlier messages are no longer in the log. Export to keep a record.
		</p>
	}
	if len(view.Messages) == 0 {
		<p class="empty" data-testid={ EmptyTestID }>No messages yet.</p>
	} else {
		<ol class="card-list" aria-live="off" data-testid={ HistoryTestID }>
			for _, message := range view.Messages {
				@MessageRow(message)
			}
		</ol>
	}
}

// MessageRow is one message in the history list.
//
// The `<li>` carries `data-seq` and nothing else identifying. A `data-testid` here
// would be the same string on every row, and §10.2's rule is that a hook appearing
// more than once in a document selects nothing; the rows are located by
// `data-seq`, which `Chat.Seq` makes unique within a campaign.
//
// The author's name is in a `<strong>` inside the row's own metadata line rather
// than in an attribute, because a name is text a reader reads and an attribute is
// text a machine reads.
templ MessageRow(message Message) {
	<li class="card chat-message" data-seq={ strconv.FormatUint(message.Seq, 10) }>
		<p class="card-meta chat-message-meta">
			<strong class="chat-message-author">{ message.Author }</strong>
			<time datetime={ message.At.Format(time.RFC3339) }>{ clockLabel(message.At) }</time>
		</p>
		<p class="chat-message-body">{ message.Body }</p>
	</li>
}

// Compose is the send form.
//
// **Inert until phase 9**, and written against the contract it has to satisfy rather
// than left out: `internal/httpapi/edit` makes the same argument for its save
// control. The form posts to `Action` with a method the route can read, and the
// live send is phase 9's job. A form that cannot send anything yet is still the
// right markup to ship against a contract, and the alternative — omitting it — makes
// the layout of the final surface a guess.
//
// The textarea carries `.target` and so does the button: §10.6's audit walks every
// focus stop and the gate reads the **served** document, so the class has to be in
// the bytes rather than applied by the script that arrives with the behaviour.
//
// `maxlength` is in characters while the server counts bytes, so the two are not the
// same number — a four-byte rune fits four times over. The server's limit is the one
// that refuses; this one stops the common case before a round trip.
//
// The form names the panel's heading with `aria-labelledby` rather than the field's
// own label, which the `<label for>` already provides. Two names on one region is
// §7.2's rule about landmarks, and a form is not a landmark — so this one is here to
// give the landmark-like form an announcement that is not the field's bare label.
templ Compose(view ComposeView) {
	if !view.SignedIn {
		// A paragraph and not a disabled textarea. §4.7's rule that a section with
		// nothing in it is absent, and a form a reader cannot use is a focus stop
		// that does nothing — which is worse than no form, because the reader has now
		// used one.
		<p class="panel-note" data-testid="chat-compose-signed-out">
			Sign in to join the conversation.
		</p>
	} else {
		<form
			class="form chat-compose"
			method="post"
			action={ view.Action }
			aria-labelledby={ PanelHeadingID }
			data-testid={ ComposeTestID }
		>
			<div class="field">
				<label for={ ComposeFieldID }>Message</label>
				<textarea
					id={ ComposeFieldID }
					class="target"
					name="body"
					rows="2"
					maxlength={ view.Max }
					placeholder={ view.Placeholder }
					aria-describedby={ describedBy(view.Error) }
					data-testid={ ComposeFieldTestID }
				>{ view.Value }</textarea>
				<p id={ ComposeHintID } class="field-hint">Enter sends. Shift and enter starts a new line.</p>
				if view.Error != "" {
					// A paragraph and not a `role="alert"`. The response is a whole
					// document rather than an insertion, so the reader meets the refusal
					// as the page loads — and a live region that fires on load is the
					// search route's mistake one level down. The field's
					// `aria-describedby` names it, so a reader who is focused on the
					// textarea when the document arrives is told.
					//
					// Not `ui.LoadError` either: that state says "semiplane could not
					// load this page", which would be false — the page loaded and the
					// *message* was what would not be sent.
					<p id={ ComposeErrorID } class="notice notice--error" data-testid={ ComposeErrorTestID }>
						{ view.Error }
					</p>
				}
			</div>
			<button type="submit" class="target button" data-testid={ ComposeSubmitTestID }>Send</button>
		</form>
	}
}

// describedBy is the field's `aria-describedby` value.
//
// **The error's id is appended rather than substituted**, so a field always has the
// hint it carries on a successful render and a reader never loses the "Enter sends"
// line because a previous attempt failed. Two ids in one attribute is the ordinary
// form of it: the value is a space-separated list and both elements are in the
// document whenever the attribute is present.
func describedBy(refusal string) string {
	if refusal == "" {
		return ComposeHintID
	}

	return ComposeHintID + " " + ComposeErrorID
}

// Export is the "export to journal" control.
//
// A `<button>` inside the panel rather than a link, because the action writes — it
// creates a page and a revision row — and a link that writes is the pattern S-6.3
// exists to forbid. It is inert until the route and phase 9 wire it, for the reason
// the save control is.
//
// **Not rendered at all for a reader who may not export**, which is the S-8 line and
// not a styling decision: the control's presence would tell a player that an action
// exists, and a disabled button is worse than an absent one for the same reason the
// signed-out compose form is a paragraph.
templ Export(view ExportView) {
	if view.Allowed {
		<button
			type="button"
			class="target button chat-export"
			data-testid={ ExportTestID }
			data-chrome={ LogChromeHook + "-export" }
		>Export to journal</button>
		if view.Withheld > 0 {
			<p class="panel-note" data-testid="chat-export-withheld">
				{ withheldLabel(view.Withheld) }
			</p>
		}
		if view.LastPath != "" {
			<p class="panel-note" data-testid="chat-export-path">{ view.LastPath }</p>
		}
	}
}

// withheldLabel is the sentence under the export control.
//
// A count and never the messages: the count says how many lines are missing from
// the page, and the lines themselves are the secret. A GM is entitled to know a
// line was withheld; nobody is entitled to learn what it said from this sentence.
func withheldLabel(withheld int) string {
	if withheld == 1 {
		return "One secret message was left out of that page."
	}

	return "Secret messages were left out of that page."
}

// countLabel is the panel's own count line.
//
// The count of what is **on screen**, not of what the buffer holds: a reader who
// sees six lines is told six, and a count taken from the buffer's length would say
// twelve and be wrong in a way that looks like a bug in the data rather than in the
// wording.
func countLabel(messages []Message) string {
	if len(messages) == 0 {
		return "Nothing said yet."
	}

	if len(messages) == 1 {
		return "1 message."
	}

	return strconv.Itoa(len(messages)) + " messages."
}

// panelState is the panel's `data-state`, so a test asserts the state rather than
// inferring it from a count.
//
// One of `empty` or `populated`, and it is written out rather than left to the
// absence of an attribute: §10.2's hook rule has already shown that a state
// rendered half-way is invisible to an audit that only counts what is present.
func panelState(messages []Message) string {
	if len(messages) == 0 {
		return "empty"
	}

	return "populated"
}

// clockLabel is the time as a reader sees it: the wall clock, UTC.
//
// UTC rather than the reader's local time, and the reason is that this value comes
// out of a buffer that stores UTC. Converting here would make a transcript's
// timestamps read in whatever zone the reader happens to be in, and two readers of
// the same table would see two different times for one message — which is the
// failure `Message.At`'s doc comment names.
//
// `<time datetime>` carries the full instant in the markup either way, so a
// client that wants to localise has the value and does not have to reparse.
func clockLabel(at time.Time) string {
	return at.UTC().Format("15:04")
}

// The component list, asserted by the a11y test so that a component added and not
// mounted cannot be quietly forgotten.
//
// Not a `var` — the point is the constant, and a slice would be a second thing to
// keep true.
const (
	// PanelRendered is `Panel`, which is what the play page's rail mounts.
	PanelRendered = "Panel"
	// LogRendered is the live region, named so a failure can say which element was
	// missing rather than "no live region".
	LogRendered = "Log"
)